<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Almoravid Empire</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Almoravids, Almoravid dynasty, Almoravid, al-Murabitun</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Almoravid Empire was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a> Muslim empire that arose from a religious reform movement among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanhaja">Sanhaja</a> of the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a> in the eleventh century. Under leaders such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yusuf-ibn-tashfin">Yusuf ibn Tashfin</a> it conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a>, founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marrakesh">Marrakesh</a>, and intervened in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> after 1086. It promoted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maliki-school">Maliki</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> and controlled trans-Saharan trade, influencing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghana-empire">Ghana Empire</a>, before it was overthrown by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohads</a> in 1147.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/almoravid-empire.jpg" alt="Almoravid Empire" width="320" height="378" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rowanwindwhistler · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Expansi%C3%B3nAlmor%C3%A1vide.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He returned to the Maghreb preaching strict tawhid, the absolute unity of God, and denouncing the ruling <b>Almoravid dynasty</b> as anthropomorphists who ascribed physical attributes to God and tolerated moral laxity.</blockquote><a href="/empire/almohad-caliphate">Almohad Caliphate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Almoravid capture of Awdaghast in 1054 reversed some sixty years of Ghanaian control over the northern trade terminus, and the Sack of Koumbi Saleh by the <b>Almoravids</b>, traditionally dated to 1076, reduced the empire for a time to a tributary of the Almoravid movement.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ghana-empire">Ghana Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The fall of Toledo prompted the taifa rulers to call in the <b>Almoravids</b> of North Africa, who defeated Alfonso at the Battle of Sagrajas in 1086 and again at the Battle of Uclés in 1108, where his only son Sancho was killed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-castile">Kingdom of Castile · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Almoravids</b> and Almohads ruled the Maghreb and al-Andalus in the eleventh and twelfth centuries; the Saadi sultans destroyed the Portuguese Aviz dynasty at the Battle of Alcácer Quibir in 1578 and crushed the Songhay Empire in 1591; and the Alawi dynasty, which seized power in 1631, reigns…</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-morocco">Kingdom of Morocco · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Garamantes of the Fezzan are often counted among their ancestors, and some researchers tie the rise of the Tuareg to the Garamantes’ fall; the Lamtuna, the core of the <b>Almoravid dynasty</b>, were close kin.</blockquote><a href="/culture/tuareg">Tuareg · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Its fall marked the deterioration of the <b>Almoravids</b>' northern front.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-18">18 December in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-house"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Rulers and members</h2><figure class="pp-chart"><p class="cty-scale" aria-hidden="true"><span>1025</span><span>1100</span><span>1175</span></p><ol class="cty-tl pp-tl"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/yahya-ibn-ibrahim"><span class="pp-av"></span><span class="pp-nm"><span class="cty-n">Yahya ibn Ibrahim</span><span class="cty-y">d. 1048</span></span></a><span class="cty-track" aria-hidden="true"><span class="cty-bar pp-dim" style="left:15.3%;width:0.8%"></span></span></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/abu-bakr-ibn-umar"><img class="pp-av" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/abu-bakr-ibn-umar.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="28" height="28"><span class="pp-nm"><span class="cty-n">Abu Bakr ibn Umar</span><span class="cty-y">d. 1087</span></span></a><span class="cty-track" aria-hidden="true"><span class="cty-bar pp-dim" style="left:41.3%;width:0.8%"></span></span></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/ali-ibn-yusuf"><img class="pp-av" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ali-ibn-yusuf.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="28" height="28"><span class="pp-nm"><span class="cty-n">Ali ibn Yusuf</span><span class="cty-y">r. 1106–1143</span></span></a><span class="cty-track" aria-hidden="true"><span class="cty-bar pp-dim" style="left:54.0%;width:24.7%"></span></span></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/tashfin-ibn-ali"><span class="pp-av"></span><span class="pp-nm"><span class="cty-n">Tashfin ibn Ali</span><span class="cty-y">d. 1145</span></span></a><span class="cty-track" aria-hidden="true"><span class="cty-bar pp-dim" style="left:80.0%;width:0.8%"></span></span></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/ishaq-ibn-ali"><span class="pp-av"></span><span class="pp-nm"><span class="cty-n">Ishaq ibn Ali</span><span class="cty-y">d. 1147</span></span></a><span class="cty-track" aria-hidden="true"><span class="cty-bar pp-dim" style="left:81.3%;width:0.8%"></span></span></li></ol><figcaption>5 people of the encyclopedia whose Wikidata record names this house as thei